# Anything that is executed inside a Linux container must check out LF, even on
# a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true. A CR at end-of-line survives into the
# shell and the failure is ugly to diagnose: `$'\r': command not found`, or a
# script that reports "cannot execute: required file not found" because its
# shebang reads `#!/bin/bash\r`. That cost a full 25-minute trial once, and the
# run still printed a plausible-looking 0.00 rather than an error.
#
# harness/*.py embeds shell snippets in Python string literals, so it is exposed
# to exactly this even though Python itself tolerates CRLF.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
